    {
      "candidate_ids": ["fe69b1dbb4d976f830cd"],
      "question": "Is m206's unusually early 150 mg 1/1 onset also present at low loading? The 50 mg 1/1 candidate predicts 8.00/20.22 mL. Together with observed 50 mg 2/1 and 150 mg 1/1, it supplies a controlled dose contrast and a low-dose solvent slope. This is more discriminating than assigning the m205/m206 difference to hydroxyl accessibility."
    },
    {
      "candidate_ids": ["81507d463506a9e65e73", "bc2a96424966b655227f", "c6924324ddd95f249df6"],
      "question": "Does the acquired m13 response transfer toward stronger eluent or to related acetoacetate connectivity? At 168.3 mg, m13 currently predicts 11.32/26.36 mL at 1/1 and 9.41/18.31 in pure EA, compared with measured 9.04/18.17 at 2/1. The predicted local maximum is untested. A different, complementary question is m107 at 5/1 and 54.5 mg, predicted 24.24/52.13, which extends its measured same-dose 2/1 setup. These alternatives distinguish within-graph shape from transfer between linked methacrylate/acetoacetate and methoxyethyl acetoacetate."
    },
    {
      "candidate_ids": ["5df546d9e2f2a29dfc89", "3f6b817d855e67b3b406", "0bdd638e906bf11a00cb"],
      "question": "Are elevated pure-EA completions being transferred too broadly? At unchanged setup, m164 predicts 5.44/35.65 mL in pure EA but 6.88/12.18 at 1/1; measured 2/1 completion is 9.90. m165 likewise predicts 6.39/22.21 in pure EA versus measured 1/1 completion of 10.04. General solvent-strength knowledge makes these prospective completion maxima worth testing, but measured exceptions elsewhere prevent declaring them erroneous. A focused strong-eluent contrast has a clearer question than another routine conjugated-ketone extension."
    },
    {
      "candidate_ids": ["8fb287430f3df6d0b3dd", "862163fa99660a023b6f"],
      "question": "How does the explicitly novel phosphorus graph m202 behave? A fixed 122.3 mg/DCM 300 uL pure-EA to 5/1 pair predicts 4.13/7.17 → 5.87/16.59 mL. Its SMILES contains formal positive charge on phosphorus and no counterion. Do not reinterpret it as a neutral phosphate or phosphine oxide. A label could test a genuinely different graph environment, but novelty alone does not establish distribution-wide learning value, and chemical interpretation is limited by the supplied identity representation."
